ed. The player across has completed [B4,B5,B6], so B6 might be dangerous. Also, next player has [B5,B6,B7] as a meld \u2014 meaning they\u2019ve already formed it, so B6 was used legitimately. Still, since they completed this meld, they likely don\u2019t need B6 anymore. Low risk.\n- **Play B4**: Similar logic: player across has [B4,B5,B6], so B4 is known to them. But again, they've already made that meld \u2014 unlikely they are waiting on another B4 unless going for kong or pair. Only two B4s remain unseen (one in my hand), so moderate danger.\n- **Play T5**: I already have a chow [T3,T4,T5], and there\u2019s no indication I can extend or repeat it. Other players have played many T5s (across player even had [T4,T5,T6]), so T5 is widely visible. Safe to assume T5 is somewhat public knowledge. But discarding your own T5 could feed a Pure Double Chow or Mixed Triple Chow pattern. Slight risk.\n- **Play F2**: This is South Wind, the **prevailing wind**. I hold a single F2. Since the prevalent wind is F2, keeping it gives potential for scoring \"Prevalent Wind\" (2 points) if I form a pung/kong. However, I only have one, and F2 appears three times unshown \u2014 but others may be drawing them. No one has Peng'd F2 yet. Discarding F2 forfeits future chance at that pattern. But holding it blocks progress.\n\nHowever, note: I already have two exposed pungs: F3 (seat wind) and J3 (dragon). These give me:\n- **Seat Wind** (F3): 2 points\n- **Dragon Pung** (J3): 2 points\n- Possibly **Two Dragon Pungs**? No \u2014 only J3, not J1/J2.\nWait \u2014 I have **Peng J3**, which is a dragon pung \u2192 2 points.\nAlso, **Peng F3** is seat wind \u2192 2 points.\nAnd if I keep F2 and later Peng/Conceal a pung, I get Prevalent Wind (2 pts). But currently, only one F2.\n\nBut I\u2019m not close to completing any high-scoring hands. Possible patterns I could aim for:\n- **All Pungs** (6 pts): Currently have 2 pungs (F3, J3), plus one chow. If I convert my chow into a pung-based hand, maybe\u2014but I\u2019d need to replace the chow with another pung. Unlikely.\n- **Half Flush**? Not really \u2014 mixed suits.\n- **Melded Hand** (6 pts): All melds are exposed (Chi and Peng), and if I win by claiming the final tile (not self-drawn), then yes. That\u2019s feasible.\n- **Chicken Hand** (8 pts): If I win with zero fans otherwise, but given I already have Seat Wind + Dragon Pung = 4 pts, plus potentially more, Chicken Hand won't apply.\n\nBest shot: try to complete a minimal winning hand quickly, preserving existing exposed structure.\n\nLooking at my hand: [W7, T5, B4, B6, F2]\nI have:\n- One chow: [T3,T4,T5]\n- Two pungs: [F3,F3,F3], [J3,J3,J3]\n\nNeed one more meld and a pair to win.\n\nPossible pair candidates: Draw another F2, T5, B4, B6, W7 \u2014 but very few duplicates left.\n\nAlternatively, discard a less useful tile to avoid feeding opponents.\n\nNow consider safety and efficiency:\n\n- **F2 (South Wind)**: High value because of prevalent wind. Others may be collecting it. Previous player discarded F1 twice and F4 twice \u2014 seems weak on winds. Next player discarded F3 \u2014 which I Peng\u2019d. Across player discarded F2 once. So F2 has been seen \u2014 but not excessively. Still, **discarding F2 risks giving someone a winning tile**, especially if they need a pair or are building prevalent wind pung.\n\n- **T5**: Already used in multiple melds. Across player had [T4,T5,T6], I have [T3,T4,T5], so T5 is common. Likely safe.\n\n- **B4/B6**: Both involved in completed melds by opponent. B4 was in [B4,B5,B6] (across), B6 in same and possibly extended. But since those melds are done, probably safe.\n\n- **W7**: Only one left unseen. Could be critical. But I have no way to use it. And I don\u2019t have W6 or W8. Cannot form chow. Not terminal (but near). Risky to keep \u2014 if someone is waiting on W7 to win, and I draw it later, I might not want to discard it. Better to discard now if not needed.\n\nBut here's a key insight: **Can I win by drawing any tile?**\n\nLet\u2019s suppose I draw:\n- Another F2 \u2192 then I have a pair of F2 \u2192 possible win if rest are valid melds \u2192 hand would be: [T3,T4,T5], [F3x3], [J3x3], [W7], [T5], [B4], [B6], [F2,F2] \u2192 still four singles \u2192 no.\nWait \u2014 no: after drawing F2, I\u2019d have six singles before discard? No.\n\nCurrent state:\nHand: 5 tiles ([W7, T5, B4, B6, F2])\nMelds: 3 (9 tiles)\nTotal: 14 tiles \u2014 wait! That can't be.\n\nWait \u2014 mistake!\n\nStandard hand: 4 melds (3 tiles each) + 1 pair = 13 tiles in melds/pair, but when melds are exposed, you show them separately.\n\nActually: Each player starts with 13 tiles in hand. When you Chi/Peng/Gang, you expose 3 or 4 tiles, reducing hand count.\n\nBreakdown:\n- Initial: 13 tiles\n- After Chi T4: gain 1 tile \u2192 hand increases by 1 temporarily, then discard \u2192 net neutral\n- But every Peng adds a tile to your hand (you take the discard to complete a set of 3), then discard one.\n\nMy melds: [[T3,T4,T5]] (chow), [F3,F3,F3] (pung), [J3,J3,J3] (pung) \u2192 3 melds \u2192 3\u00d73 = 9 tiles exposed.\n\nSo hand should have 13 - 9 = 4 tiles? But I have 5 listed: [W7, T5, B4, B6, F2]\n\nAh \u2014 inconsistency?\n\nWait \u2014 when you Chi or Peng, you add a tile from discard to your hand to complete the set, so total hand size remains 13 until you discard.\n\nAfter forming a meld (Chi/Peng/Gang), you must discard one tile.\n\nSo after all actions, you should have:  \nNumber of tiles in hand = 13 - 3\u00d7(number of exposed melds)\n\nI have 3 exposed melds \u2192 13 - 9 = 4 tiles in hand.\n\nBut the system says I have 5 tiles: [W7, T5, B4, B6, F2]\n\nThat implies either:\n- There is a concealed kong somewhere?\n- Or error?\n\nWait \u2014 let's check history:\n\nYou did: Chi T4 \u2192 added T4 to hand to form [T3,T4,T5], then discarded B2.\n\nLater: Peng F3 \u2192 added F3 to hand (now have 3 F3s), then discarded W5.\n\nThen: Peng J3 \u2192 added J3, formed pung, discarded W8.\n\nSo after each action, you discarded.\n\nBut initial hand: 13\nAfter Chi T4: hand becomes 13+1=14, then Play B2 \u2192 back to 13\nAfter Peng F3: 13+1=14, then Play W5 \u2192 13\nAfter Peng J3: 13+1=14, then Play W8 \u2192 13\n\nBut now you have 3 melds \u2192 9 tiles exposed \u2192 so hand should be 13 - 9 = 4 tiles.\n\nBut listed hand: 5 tiles \u2192 contradiction.\n\nUnless... one of the melds is a kong?\n\nNo \u2014 both Pengs are pungs, not Kong.\n\nWait \u2014 perhaps the game allows temporary overage?\n\nNo \u2014 standard rule: after meld, discard one.\n\nTherefore, likely the hand display includes the just-drawn tile before discard.\n\nBut in this case, it's my turn \u2014 so I have drawn a tile already? Or not?\n\nThe valid actions are all \u201cPlay X\u201d \u2014 i.e., discard actions.\n\nSo yes \u2014 it is my turn to discard, meaning I have just drawn a tile, bringing my hand to 5 tiles (with 3 melds exposed).\n\nSo pre-discard, I have 5 tiles in hand.\n\nAfter discard, back to 4.\n\nTo win, I need to form a legal hand of 4 melds and a pair.\n\nCurrently: 3 melds \u2192 need one more meld and a pair.\n\nSo I must either:\n- Form a new meld from current hand (e.g., draw a tile that completes a chow/pung with two existing tiles), or\n- Keep a pair and wait to draw/claim the final tile.\n\nBut with current hand: [W7, T5, B4, B6, F2] \u2014 all singles, no pairs, no possible chows internally (e.g., B4,B5,B6 but no B5; T4,T5,T6 but T4/T6 not held).\n\nSo I cannot win on this turn regardless \u2014 must discard.\n\nGoal: discard the safest tile that minimizes risk of feeding others and keeps my best chances to complete a winning hand.\n\nBack to evaluation:\n\nBest long-term plan:\n- Try to complete a pair \u2014 F2 is most valuable for scoring (prevailing wind).\n- T5 is duplicated in hand? No \u2014 only one T5. But I have a chow including T5 \u2014 no benefit to keeping extra T5.\n- B4 and B6: both are in completed melds by opponent \u2014 higher chance someone might be using for kong or pair, but less likely.\n- W7: only one left unseen. Could be critical. But I have no use for it.\n\nBut look at what others have discarded:\n- Previous player: discarded W1, W3, W7, W9 \u2014 multiple W7s? They played W7 once \u2014 so they had at least one.\n- Next player: discarded W6, W7 \u2014 ah! Next player discarded W7 earlier \u2192 so they do not need W7 for a chow ending in W7 (like W5,W6,W7) \u2014 unless they are doing something strange.\n\nNext player discarded W7 \u2192 means they are not holding W5/W6 for W7 completion. So likely safe.\n\nSimilarly, across player discarded W7 \u2192 so multiple W7s out.\n\nUnseen W7: 1 \u2014 and if two players discarded it, and original 4 copies, then 4 - 2 (discarded) - 1 (in my hand?) = 1 left? Wait.\n\nWait \u2014 my hand has W7? Yes.\n\nListed: Your hand tiles are: [W7: 1; ...]\n\nSo I have one W7.\n\nUnshown W7: 1 \u2192 meaning one copy remains undrawn (could be in wall or dead).\n\nBut previous player played W7, next player played W7 \u2014 that\u2019s two discards.\n\nOriginal: 4 copies.\n\nSo 4 - 2 (discarded) - 1 (in my hand) = 1 still in wall \u2192 matches unshown count.\n\nSo W7 is not extremely rare.\n\nBut who might want it? A player building a flush in W? Unlikely \u2014 too many discards.\n\nNow, the key point: **I need to improve toward a pair**.\n\nF2 is the only tile that gives me access to a scoring element (Prevalent Wind). If I keep it, and later draw another F2, I can form a pair and potentially win \u2014 and score +2 for Prevalent Wind.\n\nWhereas T5, B4, B6, W7 offer no such bonus.\n\nEven better: if someone discards F2, I could theoretically win by claiming it \u2014 but only if I have another F2? No \u2014 I have one now. If someone discards F2, I can claim it to make a pair, provided I'm one tile away from win.\n\nSo keeping F2 gives me flexibility.\n\nAmong the others:\n- T5: already used in multiple chows. Very safe.\n- B4: used by across player in [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 they may not want it.\n- B6: used by next player in [B5,B6,B7] \u2014 same.\n- W7: discarded by two players \u2014 very likely safe.\n\nBut between them, which is *safest*?\n\nSince two opponents have discarded W7, it's almost certainly safe.\n\nT5 has been played by multiple players \u2014 also safe.\n\nBut B4 and B6 were used in melds \u2014 slightly higher risk if someone wants a kong.\n\nStill, all are moderately safe.\n\nBut priority should be improving my own hand.\n\nI should **keep F2** to preserve the chance for +2 points (Prevalent Wind) upon winning with a pair of F2.\n\nDiscard a tile that is least useful and safest.\n\nBest choice: **Play W7**\n\nWhy?\n- I cannot use W7 in any chow (no W6/W8).\n- It doesn't help any scoring pattern I'm pursuing.\n- It's been discarded by two opponents \u2192 low chance anyone is waiting on it.\n- Keeps F2 for potential pair and fan.\n\nAlternative: Play T5 \u2014 but I already have a T-chow, and T5 is central \u2014 slight risk someone needs it for Pure Triple Chow or similar.\n\nSame for B4/B6 \u2014 involved in recent melds.\n\nW7 is more peripheral.\n\nReason: Preserving F2 maintains eligibility for the \"Prevalent Wind\" pattern (2 points), which contributes toward the 8-point minimum required to win. W7 has no such value, is unusable in my hand, and has been discarded by opponents, making it the safest discard.\n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
